    {
      "title": "Uncovered Interest Parity (UIP) Components",
      "source_segment_ids": [
        "CON_0021",
        "FCT_0066",
        "FCT_0067",
        "FCT_0068",
        "FCT_0069",
        "FCT_0070"
      ],
      "mode": "visual_only",
      "remaining_text": null,
      "visuals": [
        {
          "source_segment_ids": [
            "CON_0021",
            "FCT_0066",
            "FCT_0067",
            "FCT_0068",
            "FCT_0069",
            "FCT_0070"
          ],
          "needs_review": false,
          "missing_information": [],
          "evidence": [
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0021",
              "exact_quote": "The uncovered interest parity is the extra equation needed when an endogenous variable such as a floating exchange rate is introduced."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0066",
              "exact_quote": "The exchange rate is floating."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0067",
              "exact_quote": "And in the denominator, you have the foreign interest rate."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0068",
              "exact_quote": "Well, remember, I can divide this by the exchange rate on both sides."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0069",
              "exact_quote": "And then what you get is 1 equal to a numerator that has the nominal exchange rate times the expected appreciation of the currency plus."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0070",
              "exact_quote": "And so when you compare the two, you have to compare one base interest rate, the domestic of the foreign, plus expected appreciation of the appreciation of that currency."
            }
          ],
          "payload": {
